(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Robotics & Mechatronics"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Robotics & Mechatronics"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Robotics and Mechatronics: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nShow formulas, substitute numbers and give units (angles in degrees or radians as stated). All numerical answers below were computed and checked in Python (NumPy, SciPy and the python-control library). Draw a labelled sketch for every kinematics question.\n\n# Assignment 1: Spatial Description and Manipulator Kinematics (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Rotations and transforms (18 marks)** (a) Rotate the vectors (1, 0, 0) and (0, 0, 1) about the y axis by 60° [4]. (b) Let M = Rz(45°) Rx(30°). Compute M, show that MᵀM = I and det M = 1, and find M applied to (0, 1, 0). Repeat with the product order reversed and comment [8]. (c) Frame B is obtained from A by rotating 30° about z and translating (1, 0, 0); frame C from B by rotating 60° about z and translating (0.5, 0.5, 0) (expressed in B). Find the transform from C to A and the position in A of the point (1, 0, 0) given in C [6].\n\n**Q2. Forward and inverse kinematics (20 marks)** A planar arm has l₁ = 0.6 m and l₂ = 0.4 m. (a) Find the tip position for θ₁ = 40°, θ₂ = 60° [4]. (b) Find the workspace limits and its area [4]. (c) Solve the inverse kinematics for the target (0.7, 0.5) m, giving both solutions [8]. (d) Verify one solution with forward kinematics and explain how you choose between them [4].\n\n**Q3. Jacobian, velocity and statics (20 marks)** For the arm of Q2 at θ₁ = 40°, θ₂ = 60°: (a) Write J and compute det J [6]. (b) Find the tip velocity for q̇ = (1, -0.5) rad/s [3]. (c) Find the joint torques needed to hold tip forces (5, 0) N and (0, -20) N [4]. (d) Find the joint rates for a tip velocity (0.1, 0) m/s here and at θ₂ = 5°, and comment [7].\n\n**Q4. Three-link planar arm (8 marks)** Links 0.5, 0.4 and 0.2 m, joint angles 30°, 30°, 30°. Compute the tip position and orientation [6] and state the number of DOF of the end-effector pose in the plane [2].\n\n**Q5. Concepts (4 marks)** What is a singularity and why is it dangerous? What is gimbal lock?\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Ry(θ) = [[cos θ, 0, sin θ], [0, 1, 0], [-sin θ, 0, cos θ]]. (1, 0, 0) → (cos 60°, 0, -sin 60°) = **(0.5, 0, -0.866)**; (0, 0, 1) → (sin 60°, 0, cos 60°) = **(0.866, 0, 0.5)**.\n(b) Step 1: M = Rz(45°) Rx(30°) = [[0.7071, -0.6124, 0.3536], [0.7071, 0.6124, -0.3536], [0, 0.5, 0.8660]].\nStep 2: Each column has unit length and columns are mutually orthogonal, so MᵀM = I; det M = +1.\nStep 3: M(0, 1, 0)ᵀ = the second column = **(-0.612, 0.612, 0.5)**.\nStep 4: Reversed order Rx(30°) Rz(45°) gives (-0.707, 0.612, 0.354) for the same vector: a **different** result, because 3D rotations do not commute.\n(c) Step 1: ᴬT_B has R = Rz(30°), p = (1, 0, 0); ᴮT_C has R = Rz(60°), p = (0.5, 0.5, 0).\nStep 2: The product has rotation Rz(90°) and translation ᴬp = (1, 0, 0) + Rz(30°)(0.5, 0.5, 0) = (1 + 0.183, 0.683, 0) = **(1.183, 0.683, 0)**.\nStep 3: The point (1, 0, 0) in C: rotate by Rz(90°) to (0, 1, 0) and add the translation: **(1.183, 1.683, 0)** in A.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) x = 0.6 cos 40° + 0.4 cos 100° = 0.4596 - 0.0695 = **0.390 m**; y = 0.6 sin 40° + 0.4 sin 100° = 0.3857 + 0.3939 = **0.780 m**.\n(b) Reach is between |l₁ - l₂| = **0.2 m** and l₁ + l₂ = **1.0 m** (an annulus, assuming unrestricted joints). Area = π(1.0² - 0.2²) = **3.02 m²**.\n(c) Step 1: Target distance = √(0.49 + 0.25) = 0.860 m (inside the workspace). cos θ₂ = (0.74 - 0.36 - 0.16)/(2 × 0.6 × 0.4) = 0.22/0.48 = 0.4583, so θ₂ = ±62.72°.\nStep 2: Elbow-down (+): θ₁ = atan2(0.5, 0.7) - atan2(0.4 sin 62.72°, 0.6 + 0.4 cos 62.72°) = 35.54° - 24.41° = **11.13°**.\nStep 3: Elbow-up (-): θ₁ = 35.54° + 24.41° = **59.95°**, θ₂ = **-62.72°**.\n(d) Forward kinematics of (11.13°, 62.72°): x = 0.6 cos 11.13° + 0.4 cos 73.85° = 0.5887 + 0.1113 = 0.700; y = 0.6 sin 11.13° + 0.4 sin 73.85° = 0.1158 + 0.3842 = 0.500 ✓. Choose by joint limits, obstacle clearance, the current configuration (smallest joint motion) and avoidance of singularities.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) J = [[-0.6 sin 40° - 0.4 sin 100°, -0.4 sin 100°], [0.6 cos 40° + 0.4 cos 100°, 0.4 cos 100°]] = [[-0.780, -0.394], [0.390, -0.0695]]. det J = l₁ l₂ sin θ₂ = 0.24 sin 60° = **0.208**.\n(b) v = J q̇ = (-0.780 + 0.197, 0.390 + 0.035) = **(-0.583, 0.425) m/s**.\n(c) τ = Jᵀ F: for (5, 0): (-3.90, -1.97) N·m; for (0, -20): (-7.80, +1.39) N·m. (Signs show direction of the required joint torques.)\n(d) Step 1: At θ₂ = 60°: q̇ = J⁻¹(0.1, 0) = **(-0.033, -0.188) rad/s**.\nStep 2: At θ₂ = 5° the arm is nearly stretched: det J = 0.0209, and q̇ = **(1.35, -3.55) rad/s**, about 20 times larger.\nStep 3: Near a singularity small tip motions demand huge joint speeds (and the tip cannot move along the arm's axis): avoid or use damped least squares.\n\n**Q4.** Angles accumulate: 30°, 60°, 90°. x = 0.5 cos 30° + 0.4 cos 60° + 0.2 cos 90° = 0.433 + 0.200 + 0 = **0.633 m**; y = 0.5 sin 30° + 0.4 sin 60° + 0.2 sin 90° = 0.25 + 0.346 + 0.2 = **0.796 m**; orientation = 30° + 30° + 30° = **90°**. The planar end-effector pose has 3 DOF (x, y, φ), so a 3R arm can set all three (a non-redundant arm).\n\n**Q5.** A singularity is a configuration where the Jacobian loses rank (det J = 0): the end-effector cannot move in some direction regardless of joint rates, and near it required joint rates and forces become very large. Gimbal lock is the loss of one rotational DOF in Euler-angle representations when two rotation axes align (for example pitch = ±90°), making the angle parameters non-unique; quaternions avoid it.\n\n# Assignment 2: Actuators, Sensors, Dynamics and Trajectories (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. DC motor (18 marks)** A motor has V = 24 V, R = 2 Ω, Kt = Ke = 0.08 N·m/A, J = 5×10⁻⁵ kg·m². (a) Find the no-load speed in rpm, stall torque and stall current [4]. (b) For a 0.3 N·m load find current, speed, mechanical output, input power, efficiency and copper loss [8]. (c) Find the mechanical time constant [2]. (d) At what torque and speed is the output power maximum, and what is that power? [4]\n\n**Q2. Gearing (12 marks)** (a) A 30:1 gearbox with 85 percent efficiency is driven by a motor producing 0.12 N·m at 4,000 rpm; find output torque and speed [4]. (b) A load of 0.9 kg·m² is attached to the output shaft; find the inertia reflected to the motor and compare it with the motor's rotor inertia of 5×10⁻⁵ kg·m² [4]. (c) What motor torque is needed to produce 6 N·m at the output? [2] (d) Name two drawbacks of high gear ratios [2].\n\n**Q3. Stepper and encoder (10 marks)** (a) A 1.8° stepper uses 1/8 microstepping: how many steps per revolution, and what speed results from 6,400 pulses/s? [4] (b) The stepper turns a lead screw of 5 mm pitch; what pulse rate gives 20 mm/s? [2] (c) A 2,000-line quadrature encoder on a wheel of radius 0.04 m: counts per revolution, angular resolution and distance per count [4].\n\n**Q4. Trajectories (20 marks)** A joint must move 60° in 1.5 s starting and finishing at rest. (a) Give the cubic polynomial coefficients, peak velocity and peak acceleration [6]. (b) Give the peak velocity and acceleration of the quintic and the position at mid-time for both [6]. (c) A trapezoidal profile with v_max = 80 °/s and a = 160 °/s²: find the ramp time and total time [4]. (d) For a 20° move with the same limits find the profile type, peak speed and time [4].\n\n**Q5. Dynamic torque (10 marks)** A uniform 1.2 kg link of length 0.5 m (mass centre at 0.25 m) carries a 0.8 kg payload at its tip. (a) Find the gravity torque at the joint when the link is horizontal [4]. (b) Find the torque at 60° above horizontal [3]. (c) Why include a safety factor when selecting the motor? [3]\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) ω₀ = V/Ke = 24/0.08 = 300 rad/s = **2,865 rpm**. T_stall = Kt V/R = 0.08 × 24/2 = **0.96 N·m**; I_stall = 24/2 = **12 A**.\n(b) I = T/Kt = 0.3/0.08 = **3.75 A**; ω = (V - I R)/Ke = (24 - 7.5)/0.08 = 206.25 rad/s = **1,970 rpm**. P_out = 0.3 × 206.25 = **61.9 W**; P_in = V I = 24 × 3.75 = **90 W**; efficiency = **68.8 percent**; copper loss = I²R = 3.75² × 2 = **28.1 W**. (Friction, iron and brush losses are ignored.)\n(c) τ_m = R J/(Ke Kt) = 2 × 5×10⁻⁵/0.0064 = **15.6 ms**.\n(d) Power is maximum at half the stall torque and half the no-load speed: T = **0.48 N·m**, ω = 150 rad/s = **1,432 rpm**, P_max = 0.48 × 150 = **72 W** (efficiency is only 50 percent there, so continuous operation is usually at lower torque).\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) T_out = 0.12 × 30 × 0.85 = **3.06 N·m**; speed = 4,000/30 = **133 rpm**.\n(b) J_reflected = 0.9/30² = **0.001 kg·m²**, which is **20 times** the rotor inertia (5×10⁻⁵): the reflected load inertia dominates. A load-to-rotor ratio above roughly 10:1 makes the loop harder to tune and sensitive to load changes, so choose a higher gear ratio (reflected inertia falls with N²), a motor with more rotor inertia, or a stiffer, lower-inertia load.\n(c) T_motor = 6/(30 × 0.85) = **0.235 N·m**.\n(d) Backlash and elasticity; friction and efficiency loss; low output speed; the motor spins fast so back-drivability is lost (the load cannot move the motor), noise and wear.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) 360/1.8 = 200 full steps × 8 = **1,600 steps/rev**; 6,400 pulses/s ÷ 1,600 = 4 rev/s = **240 rpm**.\n(b) 20 mm/s ÷ 5 mm/rev = 4 rev/s × 1,600 = **6,400 pulses/s**.\n(c) 2,000 × 4 = **8,000 counts/rev**; resolution 360/8,000 = **0.045°**; distance per count = 2π(0.04)/8,000 = **0.0314 mm**.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) a₂ = 3Δq/T² = 3 × 60/2.25 = **80 °/s²**; a₃ = -2Δq/T³ = -120/3.375 = **-35.56 °/s³**. Peak velocity = 1.5 Δq/T = **60 °/s** (at mid-time); peak acceleration = 6Δq/T² = **160 °/s²** (at the ends, a discontinuity).\n(b) Quintic: peak velocity = 1.875 Δq/T = **75 °/s**; peak acceleration = 5.77 Δq/T² = **154 °/s²** (smooth, continuous acceleration). At t = T/2 both are at the midpoint **30°** (symmetry).\n(c) Ramp time t_b = v_max/a = 80/160 = **0.5 s**; distance in the ramps = v²/a = 40°, which is less than 60°, so a trapezoid applies; cruise distance 20° at 80 °/s = 0.25 s; total = 0.5 + 0.25 + 0.5 = **1.25 s** (= Δq/v + v/a).\n(d) The ramps need 40° but the move is only 20°: **triangular** profile. Peak speed = √(Δq a) = √(20 × 160) = **56.6 °/s**; time = 2√(Δq/a) = 2√(0.125) = **0.707 s**.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) τ = (1.2 × 0.25 + 0.8 × 0.5) × 9.81 = (0.3 + 0.4) × 9.81 = **6.87 N·m**.\n(b) Torque scales with the cosine of the angle from horizontal: 6.87 cos 60° = **3.43 N·m**.\n(c) Real loads add acceleration torque (J α), friction, payload and model uncertainty, supply-voltage sag, temperature, aging, and uncertainties in the weight estimate; a factor of 1.5 to 2 on torque (and a thermal check on RMS torque) prevents overheating and stalling.\n\n# Assignment 3: Mobile Robots, Control, Estimation and Planning (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Differential drive (14 marks)** r = 0.1 m, b = 0.5 m, ω_L = 6 rad/s, ω_R = 8 rad/s. (a) Find v, ω and the turning radius [4]. (b) Integrate the pose for 2 s using four Euler steps of 0.5 s starting from the origin, and compare with the exact arc [6]. (c) For a pivot turn with ω_L = -4 and ω_R = +4, find ω and the time for 180° [4].\n\n**Q2. Ackermann and camera (10 marks)** (a) A car-like robot has wheelbase 0.6 m; find the turning radius for a steering angle of 25° and the steering angle for a 2 m radius [4]. (b) A camera with f = 600 px and principal point (320, 240) sees a point (-0.4, 0.3, 1.5) m; find its pixel coordinates [3]. (c) Stereo: f = 600 px, baseline 0.1 m, disparity 15 px; find the depth and the depth error for a one-pixel disparity error [3].\n\n**Q3. Control (14 marks)** (a) Find ζ for 10 percent overshoot [3]. (b) A second-order loop with ω_n = 8 rad/s: for ζ = 0.4, 0.6 and 0.8 give the percent overshoot and the settling time (about 4/(ζω_n)) [5]. (c) A speed loop has G = 150/(0.04s + 1) with proportional gain 0.1: find the closed-loop DC gain, time constant and steady-state error [4]. (d) How do you remove the steady-state error? [2]\n\n**Q4. Estimation (12 marks)** (a) One-dimensional Kalman filter with x = 0, P = 2, Q = 0.2, R = 1, motion 2 m per step and measurements 1.8, 4.3, 6.1: compute the gain, estimate and variance for each step [9]. (b) Why does the gain fall? [3]\n\n**Q5. Occupancy grid (6 marks)** A cell starts at p = 0.5. Sensor readings give p = 0.8 (occupied), 0.8, 0.3 (free) and 0.8. Compute the cell probability after each reading using log-odds.\n\n**Q6. Planning (14 marks)** Grid (S start, G goal, # wall; 4-connected moves of cost 1):\n```\nS...#...\n....#.#.\n..#...#.\n.##.#...\n....#..G\n```\n(a) Find the shortest path length and give one path [6]. (b) Compare nodes expanded by A* (Manhattan heuristic) and Dijkstra [4]. (c) Is the Manhattan heuristic admissible here? Why? [2] (d) Name two drawbacks of potential-field planning [2].\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) v = r(ω_R + ω_L)/2 = 0.1 × 14/2 = **0.70 m/s**; ω = r(ω_R - ω_L)/b = 0.1 × 2/0.5 = **0.40 rad/s**; R = v/ω = **1.75 m** (the robot circles counter-clockwise).\n(b) Step 1: Euler (Δt = 0.5): step 1: x = 0.35, y = 0, θ = 0.2; step 2: x = 0.35 + 0.35 cos 0.2 = 0.693, y = 0.35 sin 0.2 = 0.0695, θ = 0.4; step 3: x = 0.693 + 0.35 cos 0.4 = 1.015, y = 0.0695 + 0.35 sin 0.4 = 0.2084, θ = 0.6; step 4: x = 1.015 + 0.35 cos 0.6 = **1.304**, y = 0.2084 + 0.35 sin 0.6 = **0.403**, θ = 0.8 rad = **45.8°**.\nStep 2: Exact arc: x = R sin(ωt) = 1.75 sin 0.8 = **1.255**, y = R(1 - cos 0.8) = **0.531**.\nStep 3: The Euler position is off by 0.05 m in x and 0.13 m in y after only 2 s: use midpoint integration or smaller steps, and fuse other sensors to bound drift.\n(c) ω = r(8)/b = 0.1 × 8/0.5 = **1.6 rad/s**; t = π/1.6 = **1.96 s**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) R = L/tan δ = 0.6/tan 25° = **1.29 m**; δ = atan(0.6/2) = **16.7°**.\n(b) u = 600(-0.4)/1.5 + 320 = **160 px**; v = 600(0.3)/1.5 + 240 = **360 px**.\n(c) Z = f B/d = 600 × 0.1/15 = **4.0 m**; with d = 14 px: 4.29 m, an error of **+0.29 m**; the error grows as Z² (Z²/(fB) per pixel).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) ζ = -ln(OS)/√(π² + ln²(OS)) = 2.303/√(9.870 + 5.302) = **0.591**.\n(b) OS = exp(-πζ/√(1-ζ²)): ζ = 0.4: **25.3 percent**, T_s ≈ 4/(0.4 × 8) = 1.25 s (exact step response 1.07 s); ζ = 0.6: **9.5 percent**, T_s ≈ 0.83 s (0.76 s exact); ζ = 0.8: **1.5 percent**, T_s ≈ 0.625 s (0.48 s exact).\n(c) Loop gain K_p K = 15; closed-loop DC gain = 15/16 = **0.9375**; time constant = 0.04/16 = **2.5 ms**; steady-state error = 1/16 = **6.25 percent**.\n(d) Add integral action (PI control) so that the error is driven to zero, with anti-windup; or use feedforward of the expected steady-state input.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: predict x = 2, P = 2.2; K = 2.2/3.2 = **0.6875**; x = 2 + 0.6875 × (1.8 - 2) = **1.8625**; P = (1 - 0.6875) × 2.2 = **0.6875**. Step 2: predict x = 3.8625, P = 0.8875; K = 0.8875/1.8875 = **0.4702**; x = 3.8625 + 0.4702 × (4.3 - 3.8625) = **4.068**; P = **0.470**. Step 3: predict x = 6.068, P = 0.670; K = 0.670/1.670 = **0.4013**; x = 6.068 + 0.4013 × (6.1 - 6.068) = **6.081**; P = **0.401**.\n(b) As the estimate becomes more certain (P falls) it trusts the prediction more and each new measurement is weighted less; the gain settles to a steady-state value set by Q and R.\n\n**Q5.** Step 1: Log-odds l = ln(p/(1-p)): 0.8 → 1.386; 0.3 → -0.847.\nStep 2: Cumulative l: 1.386 → p = **0.80**; 2.773 → **0.941**; 1.925 → **0.873**; 3.312 → **0.965**.\nStep 3: The free reading lowers confidence only partly, and further occupied readings restore it.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) The shortest path has length **11**; one path: (0,0)→(0,1)→(0,2)→(0,3)→(1,3)→(2,3)→(2,4)→(2,5)→(3,5)→(3,6)→(3,7)→(4,7). The Manhattan distance is 4 + 7 = 11, so the path is as short as possible.\n(b) A* expands **25** nodes; Dijkstra **30** (uniform exploration in all directions). A good heuristic focuses the search.\n(c) Yes: with unit-cost 4-connected moves the true cost can never be less than the Manhattan distance, since each step changes row or column by at most one; walls only lengthen paths. An admissible heuristic guarantees A* returns an optimal path.\n(d) Local minima (the robot gets trapped in U-shaped obstacles or between obstacles), oscillation in narrow corridors, and no guarantee of reaching the goal; they also ignore kinematic and dynamic constraints.\n","exam":"# Robotics and Mechatronics: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\nFormulas: planar two-link arm x = l₁ cos θ₁ + l₂ cos(θ₁ + θ₂), y = l₁ sin θ₁ + l₂ sin(θ₁ + θ₂); cos θ₂ = (x² + y² - l₁² - l₂²)/(2 l₁ l₂); DC motor V = I R + Ke ω, T = Kt I, τ_m = R J/(Ke Kt); differential drive v = r(ω_R + ω_L)/2, ω = r(ω_R - ω_L)/b; overshoot = exp(-πζ/√(1-ζ²)); stereo Z = f B/d; Kalman K = P/(P + R).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** A robot differs from an automated machine mainly because it\nA) has a motor  B) uses electricity  C) has wheels  D) senses its environment and makes decisions to act\n\n**2.** The repeatability of a robot refers to\nA) how closely it returns to the same commanded pose  B) its weight  C) its maximum payload  D) its top speed\n\n**3.** The correct relation between a gearbox's output speed and torque is\nA) speed falls by the ratio and torque rises by the ratio (less losses)  B) neither changes  C) both increase  D) both decrease\n\n**4.** A stepper motor driven open loop can\nA) lose steps if overloaded  B) never lose steps  C) measure its own position  D) exceed stall torque\n\n**5.** The mechanical time constant of a DC motor is\nA) Kt/Ke  B) L/R  C) J/B²  D) R J/(Ke Kt)\n\n**6.** A homogeneous transform matrix is a\nA) 4 × 4 matrix with a rotation and a translation  B) 1 × 6 vector  C) 3 × 3 rotation only  D) 2 × 2 matrix\n\n**7.** Quaternions are preferred for interpolation of orientation because they\nA) have no singularity and interpolate smoothly (slerp)  B) are Euler angles  C) use fewer multiplications always  D) are easier to read\n\n**8.** The workspace of a planar two-link arm with unrestricted joints is\nA) an annulus between |l₁ - l₂| and l₁ + l₂  B) a cube  C) a line  D) a point\n\n**9.** The Jacobian relates\nA) mass to inertia  B) joint velocities to end-effector velocities  C) sensor noise to range  D) joint torques to currents\n\n**10.** Near a singularity the joint velocities needed for a tip velocity\nA) become negative  B) become zero  C) become very large  D) are unaffected\n\n**11.** A trapezoidal velocity profile has\nA) constant acceleration, a cruise at constant velocity, then constant deceleration  B) constant jerk  C) a sine-wave shape  D) zero velocity\n\n**12.** The damping ratio for no overshoot in a second-order system is\nA) ζ = 0  B) ζ < 0.1  C) ζ ≥ 1  D) ζ = 0.5\n\n**13.** Feedforward control in robotics is used to\nA) add noise  B) remove the need for a model  C) replace sensors  D) supply the expected torque (gravity, acceleration) so feedback only corrects errors\n\n**14.** In a differential-drive robot, unequal wheel speeds produce\nA) a curved path with radius R = v/ω  B) no motion  C) only straight motion  D) sideways motion\n\n**15.** A non-holonomic robot such as a car\nA) cannot translate sideways and needs manoeuvres to park  B) has no kinematics  C) can move in any direction instantly  D) has no wheels\n\n**16.** The Kalman gain decreases as the estimate variance\nA) is infinite  B) decreases  C) is unrelated  D) increases\n\n**17.** In an occupancy grid using log-odds, repeated \"occupied\" evidence\nA) resets the cell  B) adds, increasing the occupied log-odds  C) is ignored  D) subtracts\n\n**18.** A* with an admissible heuristic\nA) needs negative costs  B) finds an optimal path while usually expanding fewer nodes than Dijkstra  C) never terminates  D) is faster than Dijkstra but not optimal\n\n**19.** A hardware emergency stop must\nA) remove power to the actuators independently of the control software  B) work only when the network is up  C) be optional  D) depend on the robot software\n\n**20.** In ROS, long-running goals with feedback and cancellation use\nA) bags  B) topics  C) actions  D) parameters only\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Rotation order (5 marks)** Compute Rz(90°) applied to Rx(90°) applied to the vector (0, 1, 0), i.e. Rz(90°)[Rx(90°)(0, 1, 0)ᵀ], and the result with the order swapped. What does this show?\n\n**B2. Inverse kinematics (5 marks)** A planar arm has l₁ = 0.5 m and l₂ = 0.3 m. For the target (0.6, 0.2) m compute cos θ₂ and both sets of joint angles.\n\n**B3. Jacobian (5 marks)** For the same arm at θ₁ = 20°, θ₂ = 50°, det J = l₁ l₂ sin θ₂. Compute it, and explain what happens as θ₂ → 0.\n\n**B4. DC motor (5 marks)** A motor has V = 48 V, R = 1.5 Ω, Kt = Ke = 0.12 N·m/A. Find the no-load speed (rpm), stall torque and stall current.\n\n**B5. Gearbox (5 marks)** A load needs 12 N·m at 25 rpm. The motor runs at 3,000 rpm. Find the gear ratio and the motor torque needed if the gearbox efficiency is 80 percent.\n\n**B6. Trajectories (5 marks)** A joint moves 120° in 2 s from rest to rest. Give the cubic's peak velocity and peak acceleration, the quintic's peak velocity and the time of a trapezoidal move with v_max = 90 °/s and a = 180 °/s².\n\n**B7. Control specification (5 marks)** A position loop must have at most 5 percent overshoot and a 2 percent settling time of 1 s. Find the required damping ratio and the minimum natural frequency (use T_s ≈ 4/(ζ ω_n)).\n\n**B8. Odometry (5 marks)** r = 0.08 m, b = 0.4 m, ω_L = 5 rad/s, ω_R = 7 rad/s. Find v, ω and the radius of the path, and explain why odometry drifts.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 40 marks)\n\n**C1. Arm kinematics and statics (10 marks)** The arm of B2 (l₁ = 0.5 m, l₂ = 0.3 m). (a) Verify one inverse-kinematics solution for (0.6, 0.2) by forward kinematics and give the other solution [3]. (b) At θ₁ = 20°, θ₂ = 50° compute J and the tip velocity for q̇ = (0.8, 0.4) rad/s [4]. (c) Compute the joint torques needed to hold a downward 15 N tip force [3].\n\n**C2. Actuator selection (10 marks)** (a) For the motor of B4 and a 0.6 N·m load find the current, speed (rpm), output power, input power, efficiency and copper loss [5]. (b) With rotor inertia J = 10⁻⁴ kg·m² find the mechanical time constant [2]. (c) For the gear problem of B5 (ratio 120, 80 percent efficiency) check that the motor can deliver the required torque at the current limit [3].\n\n**C3. Mobile robot estimation (10 marks)** (a) For the robot of B8, integrate the pose for 1 s with two Euler steps of 0.5 s starting at the origin facing +x, and compare with the exact circular arc [5]. (b) A 1D Kalman filter has x = 1, P = 1, Q = 0.1, R = 0.4 and receives the measurement z = 1.5 (no motion): find K, the new estimate and the new variance [3]. (c) Name two sensors to fuse with odometry and why [2].\n\n**C4. Perception and planning (10 marks)** (a) A stereo camera with f = 500 px and baseline 0.2 m measures a disparity of 25 px: find the depth and the error caused by a 1-pixel disparity error [3]. (b) A cell starts at p = 0.5 and receives readings p = 0.7, 0.7, 0.7: compute the log-odds and probability after each [3]. (c) Explain why A* with the Manhattan heuristic is optimal on a 4-connected unit-cost grid and when it fails to be [4].\n\n**C5. System design of a warehouse robot (10 marks)** A 40 kg differential-drive robot has two wheels of radius 0.1 m, rolling-resistance coefficient 0.02, maximum speed 1.5 m/s, acceleration 0.5 m/s², drive efficiency 0.8, a 24 V 20 Ah battery and an average power draw of 50 W. (a) Compute the rolling resistance force and the mechanical power at constant speed [2]. (b) Compute the total force during acceleration and the peak electrical drive power [3]. (c) Estimate the battery energy and runtime at 50 W [2]. (d) The robot decelerates at 1.0 m/s² with a 0.2 s reaction latency; find the stopping distance from full speed and state the safety implication [3].\n\n## Answer key and mark scheme\n\n### Section A\n1. D 2. A 3. A 4. A 5. D 6. A 7. A 8. A 9. B 10. C 11. A 12. C 13. D 14. A 15. A 16. B 17. B 18. B 19. A 20. C\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** Rx(90°)(0, 1, 0) = (0, cos 90°, sin 90°) = (0, 0, 1); then Rz(90°)(0, 0, 1) = (0, 0, 1): result **(0, 0, 1)** [2]. Swapped: Rz(90°)(0, 1, 0) = (-1, 0, 0); Rx(90°)(-1, 0, 0) = **(-1, 0, 0)** [2]. The results differ, so 3D rotations do not commute [1].\n\n**B2.** cos θ₂ = (0.36 + 0.04 - 0.25 - 0.09)/(2 × 0.5 × 0.3) = 0.06/0.30 = **0.20**, θ₂ = ±78.46° [2]. Elbow-down (θ₂ = +78.46°): θ₁ = atan2(0.2, 0.6) - atan2(0.3 sin 78.46°, 0.5 + 0.3 cos 78.46°) = 18.43° - 27.69° = **-9.26°**. Elbow-up (θ₂ = -78.46°): θ₁ = 18.43° + 27.69° = **46.13°** [3].\n\n**B3.** det J = 0.5 × 0.3 × sin 50° = **0.115** [2]. As θ₂ → 0 the arm stretches out, det J → 0 (singularity): the tip cannot move along the arm and joint speeds for finite tip velocity grow without bound; the arm should avoid or damp this region [3].\n\n**B4.** No-load speed = V/Ke = 48/0.12 = 400 rad/s = **3,820 rpm** [2]; stall torque = Kt V/R = 0.12 × 48/1.5 = **3.84 N·m** [2]; stall current = V/R = **32 A** [1].\n\n**B5.** Ratio = 3,000/25 = **120** [2]. Motor torque = 12/(120 × 0.8) = **0.125 N·m** [3].\n\n**B6.** Cubic: peak velocity = 1.5 × 120/2 = **90 °/s**; peak acceleration = 6 × 120/4 = **180 °/s²** [2]. Quintic: peak velocity = 1.875 × 120/2 = **112.5 °/s** [1.5]. Trapezoid: ramp time 90/180 = 0.5 s; ramp distance v²/a = 45° ≤ 120°, so total time = Δq/v + v/a = 1.333 + 0.5 = **1.83 s** [1.5].\n\n**B7.** ζ = -ln(0.05)/√(π² + ln²(0.05)) = 2.996/√(9.870 + 8.974) = **0.69** [3]. ω_n ≥ 4/(ζ T_s) = 4/(0.69 × 1) = **5.8 rad/s** [2].\n\n**B8.** v = 0.08 × (5 + 7)/2 = **0.48 m/s**; ω = 0.08 × (7 - 5)/0.4 = **0.40 rad/s**; R = 0.48/0.40 = **1.2 m** [3]. Odometry integrates small errors (wheel radius and base errors, slip, encoder quantization, uneven floors) with no external correction, so the position error grows without bound [2].\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1.**\n(a) For θ₂ = +78.46°, θ₁ = -9.26°: x = 0.5 cos(-9.26°) + 0.3 cos(69.2°) = 0.4935 + 0.1065 = 0.600 ✓; y = 0.5 sin(-9.26°) + 0.3 sin(69.2°) = -0.0805 + 0.2805 = 0.200 ✓. The other solution is θ₁ = 46.13°, θ₂ = -78.46° [3].\n(b) J = [[-0.5 sin 20° - 0.3 sin 70°, -0.3 sin 70°], [0.5 cos 20° + 0.3 cos 70°, 0.3 cos 70°]] = [[-0.453, -0.282], [0.572, 0.103]] [2]. v = J q̇ = (-0.453 × 0.8 - 0.282 × 0.4, 0.572 × 0.8 + 0.103 × 0.4) = **(-0.475, 0.499) m/s** [2].\n(c) τ = Jᵀ F = Jᵀ(0, -15) = (0.572 × -15, 0.103 × -15) = **(-8.59, -1.54) N·m** [3].\n\n**C2.**\n(a) I = T/Kt = 0.6/0.12 = **5 A**; ω = (V - I R)/Ke = (48 - 7.5)/0.12 = 337.5 rad/s = **3,223 rpm**; P_out = 0.6 × 337.5 = **202.5 W**; P_in = 48 × 5 = **240 W**; efficiency = **84.4 percent**; copper loss = I²R = 25 × 1.5 = **37.5 W** [5].\n(b) τ_m = R J/(Ke Kt) = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴/0.0144 = **10.4 ms** [2].\n(c) Required motor torque 0.125 N·m needs I = 0.125/0.12 = 1.04 A, far below the 32 A stall current and any reasonable current limit: adequate; the motor speed at that load is near 3,820 rpm minus a small drop, matching the 3,000 rpm requirement with margin (the speed is set by the supply voltage) [3].\n\n**C3.**\n(a) v = 0.48, ω = 0.40. Euler steps (Δt = 0.5): step 1: x = 0.24, y = 0, θ = 0.2; step 2: x = 0.24 + 0.24 cos 0.2 = **0.475**, y = 0.24 sin 0.2 = **0.048**, θ = 0.4 rad. Exact arc (R = 1.2): x = 1.2 sin 0.4 = **0.467**, y = 1.2(1 - cos 0.4) = **0.095**. The Euler heading and y are off (0.048 versus 0.095) because the heading is updated after using the old value; midpoint integration reduces this error [5].\n(b) P = 1 + 0.1 = 1.1; K = 1.1/(1.1 + 0.4) = **0.733**; x = 1 + 0.733 × (1.5 - 1) = **1.367**; P = (1 - 0.733) × 1.1 = **0.293** [3].\n(c) IMU (gyro gives accurate short-term heading and rotation rate), LiDAR or camera (absolute landmarks, scan matching and loop closure), GNSS outdoors (global position): each has complementary error characteristics [2].\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Z = f B/d = 500 × 0.2/25 = **4.0 m**; with d = 24 px: 4.17 m, an error of **0.17 m** (grows as Z²/(fB)) [3].\n(b) l = ln(p/(1-p)) = ln(0.7/0.3) = 0.847 each: after one reading l = 0.847 (p = **0.70**); two: 1.695 (p = **0.845**); three: 2.542 (p = **0.927**) [3].\n(c) On a 4-connected unit-cost grid the Manhattan distance never overestimates the true remaining cost (admissible) and is consistent, so A* returns an optimal path. It is no longer admissible if diagonal moves are allowed (cost √2 or 1 per diagonal can make the heuristic overestimate when the diagonal costs 1), if move costs can be less than 1, or if the heuristic is scaled up; then A* may return a non-optimal path [4].\n\n**C5.**\n(a) F_roll = μ m g = 0.02 × 40 × 9.81 = **7.85 N**; mechanical power at 1.5 m/s = 7.85 × 1.5 = **11.8 W** [2].\n(b) F_total = m a + F_roll = 20 + 7.85 = **27.85 N**; mechanical power at 1.5 m/s = 41.8 W; electrical = 41.8/0.8 = **52.2 W** (per wheel: torque = 27.85 × 0.1/2 = 1.39 N·m at 143 rpm) [3].\n(c) Energy = 24 V × 20 Ah = **480 Wh**; runtime = 480/50 = **9.6 h** (derate for battery ageing, depth of discharge and temperature: use perhaps 80 percent, 7.7 h) [2].\n(d) Stopping distance = v²/(2a) + v t_r = 1.5²/2 + 1.5 × 0.2 = 1.125 + 0.30 = **1.43 m** [2]. Safety fields of the LiDAR/bumper must trigger at more than this distance (plus margins), and the speed must be reduced in crowded areas, where the required stopping distance must fit within the sensing range; the emergency stop circuit must cut drive power independently of the software [1].\n","notes":"# Robotics and Mechatronics: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nPrerequisites: linear algebra, calculus and ODEs, physics (mechanics, electricity), control systems, embedded systems, and basic probability. Every number in the worked examples (rotations, kinematics, Jacobians, motor models, trajectories, step responses, Kalman filter, A* search) was computed in Python with NumPy, SciPy and the python-control library; check them yourself and then vary the parameters.\n\n## Chapter 1. Mechatronics and Robot Systems\n\n### 1.1 What mechatronics is\n**Mechatronics** integrates mechanical engineering, electronics, control and software to build intelligent machines (robots, printers, cars, drones, medical devices). A **robot** adds autonomy: it **senses** the world, **decides** (plans, controls) and **acts** with actuators. The key skill is system-level design: choosing sensors, actuators, structure, electronics and algorithms together, because each choice constrains the others.\n\n### 1.2 Architecture of a robot\nMechanical structure (links, joints, wheels) → actuators and drives → sensors → signal conditioning and interfaces → computing (microcontroller for real-time loops, single-board computer or PC for perception and planning) → software layers: low-level control (1 kHz), state estimation (100 Hz), planning (1 to 10 Hz), mission/behaviour. Power system (battery, regulators, protection) and communication (CAN, UART, Ethernet, Wi-Fi) tie them together.\n\n### 1.3 Classes of robots\n- **Serial manipulators** (industrial arms), **parallel robots** (delta, Stewart platform), **SCARA** for assembly.\n- **Mobile robots**: wheeled (differential drive, Ackermann, omnidirectional), legged, aerial (multirotor), underwater, and tracked.\n- **Humanoids, soft robots, exoskeletons, medical and surgical robots, collaborative robots (cobots)**.\nKey specifications: degrees of freedom (DOF), workspace, payload, accuracy, repeatability, speed, and safety rating.\n\n### 1.4 Degrees of freedom and mobility\nA rigid body in space has 6 DOF. A serial arm with n single-DOF joints has n DOF; a planar 2-link arm has 2. To place a tool anywhere in 3D with any orientation you need at least 6 joints. Redundant arms (more than 6) can avoid obstacles and singularities. Grübler's formula counts mobility for mechanisms with closed loops.\n\n## Chapter 2. Sensors and Actuators\n\n### 2.1 Proprioceptive and exteroceptive sensors\n- **Joint position**: incremental encoders (quadrature gives 4 counts per line: a 1024-line encoder yields 4,096 counts/rev), absolute encoders, potentiometers, resolvers. For a wheel of radius 0.05 m one count is 2π(0.05)/4096 = **0.0767 mm**, and 1 m of travel is about **13,038 counts**.\n- **Inertial**: gyroscope (angular rate, drifts), accelerometer (specific force, noisy), magnetometer (heading, disturbed by metal); combine in an IMU with a complementary or Kalman filter.\n- **Force/torque and current** sensing for contact and compliance; **limit switches and bump sensors** for safety.\n- **Range**: ultrasonic sensors measure the echo time t: d = c t/2 with c ≈ 343 m/s (an echo after 5.8 ms is 0.995 m; a target at 2 m returns after 11.7 ms); beam widths are wide, with specular reflection errors. **Infrared** triangulation, **time-of-flight**, **2D/3D LiDAR** (angular scan of distances), **radar**.\n- **Vision**: monocular, stereo and RGB-D cameras; event cameras. GPS/GNSS outdoors, with RTK for centimetre accuracy.\n\n### 2.2 DC motors\nElectrical: V = I R + L dI/dt + Ke ω; mechanical: J dω/dt = Kt I - B ω - T_L. In SI units Kt = Ke. Example motor: V = 12 V, R = 1.2 Ω, Kt = Ke = 0.05 N·m/A (V·s/rad), J = 2×10⁻⁵ kg·m², B = 10⁻⁴ N·m·s.\n- **No-load speed** ω₀ = V/Ke = 240 rad/s = **2,292 rpm**.\n- **Stall torque** T_s = Kt V/R = 0.05 × 12/1.2 = **0.5 N·m**, with stall current V/R = **10 A** (drivers and thermal limits matter!).\n- With a 0.1 N·m load: I = T/Kt = 2 A, ω = (V - I R)/Ke = (12 - 2.4)/0.05 = 192 rad/s = **1,833 rpm**; mechanical output power 19.2 W, electrical input 24 W, efficiency 80 percent (copper loss only).\n- **Time constants**: mechanical τ_m = R J/(Ke Kt) = 1.2 × 2×10⁻⁵/0.0025 = **9.6 ms**; electrical τ_e = L/R = 1 mH/1.2 Ω = 0.83 ms (so electrical dynamics are much faster and often neglected).\n- Torque-speed line: torque falls linearly with speed; choose the operating point near maximum power at half the stall torque and half the no-load speed, and keep continuous current below the rated value.\nDrivers: **H-bridge** with PWM (typically 10 to 20 kHz) for speed and direction, with current sensing and flyback protection; brushless DC (BLDC) and PMSM motors with field-oriented control for high performance.\n\n### 2.3 Gears, steppers and servos\nA gearbox with ratio N multiplies torque and divides speed: a 50:1 gearbox on a 0.05 N·m, 3,000 rpm motor with 80 percent efficiency gives **2.0 N·m at 60 rpm**. Load inertia reflected to the motor is J_L/N²: 0.02 kg·m² becomes 8×10⁻⁶ kg·m² at the motor shaft, which is why high gear ratios make loads easy to control but add backlash and friction. A **stepper motor** with 200 steps/rev and 1/16 microstepping has 3,200 steps/rev (0.1125° per step); a 10 kHz step rate gives 10,000/3,200 × 60 = **187.5 rpm**. Steppers run open loop but lose steps if overloaded. **Hobby servos** have built-in position loops; **smart servos** report position, load and temperature.\n\n### 2.4 Holding torque and sizing\nA 2 kg payload at a 0.5 m lever arm needs a static torque m g L = 2 × 9.81 × 0.5 = **9.81 N·m** (when horizontal); at 30° above horizontal it is 9.81 cos 30° = 8.50 N·m. Add the link's own weight, acceleration torque J α, friction and a 1.5 to 2 safety factor.\n\n## Chapter 3. Spatial Description\n\n### 3.1 Rotation matrices\nA rotation matrix R is orthonormal (RᵀR = I, det R = +1). Rotation about z by θ:\n```\nRz(θ) = [[cos θ, -sin θ, 0], [sin θ, cos θ, 0], [0, 0, 1]]\n```\nRz(30°) takes (1, 0, 0) to (0.866, 0.5, 0). Rotations in 3D **do not commute**: with R_z(90°) R_x(90°) the vector (1, 2, 3) becomes (3, 1, 2), but with R_x(90°) R_z(90°) it becomes (-2, -3, 1). Order matters, and convention (fixed versus moving axes) must be stated.\n\n### 3.2 Homogeneous transforms\nA rigid transform is the 4×4 matrix T = [[R, p], [0, 1]]. For R = Rz(90°) and p = (2, 1, 0), the point (1, 0, 0) maps to (2, 2, 0); the inverse transform [[Rᵀ, -Rᵀp], [0, 1]] recovers the original point. Compose transforms by multiplication: ᴬT_C = ᴬT_B ᴮT_C.\n\n### 3.3 Orientation representations\nEuler angles (roll-pitch-yaw) are intuitive but suffer **gimbal lock**; axis-angle and **unit quaternions** (four numbers, no singularity, easy interpolation by slerp) are preferred for computation; rotation matrices for transformation. Orientation error is often computed as a rotation vector.\n\n## Chapter 4. Manipulator Kinematics\n\n### 4.1 Forward kinematics\nPlanar two-link arm with link lengths l₁ = 1.0 and l₂ = 0.8:\nx = l₁ cos θ₁ + l₂ cos(θ₁ + θ₂), y = l₁ sin θ₁ + l₂ sin(θ₁ + θ₂).\nFor θ₁ = 30°, θ₂ = 45° the tip is at **(1.073, 1.273)**. For general arms use the **Denavit-Hartenberg (DH)** convention: each joint has four parameters (a, α, d, θ), giving a transform Aᵢ; the end-effector pose is T = A₁A₂...Aₙ.\n\n### 4.2 Inverse kinematics\nGiven a target (x, y) find the joint angles. For the two-link arm, cos θ₂ = (x² + y² - l₁² - l₂²)/(2 l₁ l₂). Example target (1.2, 0.9): cos θ₂ = (2.25 - 1.0 - 0.64)/1.6 = 0.381, so θ₂ = ±67.59°. **Elbow-down** (θ₂ = +67.59°) gives θ₁ = atan2(0.9, 1.2) - atan2(0.8 sin θ₂, 1 + 0.8 cos θ₂) = **7.33°**; **elbow-up** (θ₂ = -67.59°) gives θ₁ = **66.41°**. Both solutions reach (1.2, 0.9) (verified by forward kinematics). The target must lie in the annulus |l₁ - l₂| ≤ r ≤ l₁ + l₂, here 0.2 to 1.8; the target distance 1.5 is reachable. Six-axis arms with a spherical wrist have closed-form solutions; others use numerical methods (Jacobian pseudo-inverse, damped least squares).\n\n### 4.3 The Jacobian and singularities\nThe Jacobian maps joint velocities to tip velocity: v = J(q) q̇. For the two-link arm:\n```\nJ = [[-l1 sin θ1 - l2 sin(θ1+θ2),  -l2 sin(θ1+θ2)],\n     [ l1 cos θ1 + l2 cos(θ1+θ2),   l2 cos(θ1+θ2)]]\n```\nAt θ₁ = 30°, θ₂ = 45°: J = [[-1.273, -0.773], [1.073, 0.207]], det J = l₁ l₂ sin θ₂ = 0.8 sin 45° = **0.566**. With q̇ = (0.5, -0.2) rad/s the tip velocity is (-0.482, 0.495) m/s. **Singularities** occur where det J = 0: here θ₂ = 0 (arm fully stretched) or 180° (folded); the arm loses the ability to move in one direction and joint speeds needed for tip motion become unbounded. The condition number of J measures closeness to singularity: 27.7 at θ₂ = 10° versus 2.4 at θ₂ = 90° (best dexterity).\n\n### 4.4 Statics: force and torque\nJoint torques that support a tip force F are τ = Jᵀ F. For a downward 10 N force at the configuration above, τ = Jᵀ(0, -10) = **(-10.73, -2.07) N·m**. The same Jacobian relates velocities (J) and forces (Jᵀ): a pose with a small Jacobian yields a large mechanical advantage. Manipulability = √det(JJᵀ).\n\n## Chapter 5. Dynamics and Actuator Selection\n\nThe equations of motion of an n-joint arm are M(q) q̈ + C(q, q̇) q̇ + g(q) = τ, where M is the inertia matrix, C the Coriolis/centrifugal terms and g gravity (derived with Lagrange's method or Newton-Euler recursion). For a single link, J q̈ = τ - m g L cos q - B q̇. Use the dynamics to compute required torques along a planned trajectory; size motors for peak and RMS torque, check gearbox limits and thermal limits, and include payload variation. Compensating gravity and inertia in the controller (computed-torque or feedforward) greatly improves accuracy.\n\n## Chapter 6. Trajectory Generation\n\nA path needs a time law that respects velocity, acceleration and jerk limits.\n- **Cubic polynomial** q(t) = q₀ + a₂t² + a₃t³ with zero start and end velocity: a₂ = 3Δq/T², a₃ = -2Δq/T³. For Δq = 90° and T = 2 s: a₂ = **67.5 °/s²**, a₃ = **-22.5 °/s³**; peak velocity 1.5Δq/T = **67.5 °/s**; peak acceleration 6Δq/T² = 135 °/s² (acceleration is discontinuous at the ends).\n- **Quintic** (zero velocity and acceleration at both ends): q = q₀ + Δq(10τ³ - 15τ⁴ + 6τ⁵), τ = t/T. Coefficients for the same move: a₃ = 112.5, a₄ = -84.375, a₅ = 16.875; peak velocity 1.875Δq/T = **84.4 °/s**; smoother, with continuous acceleration.\n- **Trapezoidal velocity profile**: accelerate at a, cruise at v_max, decelerate. For Δq = 90°, v_max = 60 °/s and a = 120 °/s²: the ramp time is t_b = v/a = 0.5 s, the move takes Δq/v + t_b = 1.5 + 0.5 = **2.0 s** with a 1.0 s cruise (a trapezoid applies because Δq > v²/a = 30°; otherwise the profile is triangular).\n- For Cartesian paths interpolate position and orientation (slerp), then convert with inverse kinematics; use splines through via-points, and time-optimal parametrization (TOPP) to respect actuator limits.\n\n## Chapter 7. Robot Control\n\n### 7.1 Joint control\nTypical structure: a cascaded **current (torque) loop** (fastest, kHz), a **velocity loop**, and a **position loop**. Each is a PI or PID controller; the inner loops are tuned first and set the bandwidth available to the outer loops. Add **feedforward** (velocity, acceleration, gravity) to reduce the error that feedback must remove.\n\n### 7.2 Second-order specifications\nA position loop is often approximated as ω_n²/(s² + 2ζω_n s + ω_n²). With ω_n = 10 rad/s: ζ = 0.5 gives overshoot 16.3 percent, rise time 0.167 s and settling time 0.81 s; ζ = 0.7 gives overshoot **4.6 percent**, rise time 0.209 s, settling 0.60 s; ζ = 1 (critical damping) gives no overshoot, rise 0.335 s, settling 0.59 s (computed). Overshoot = exp(-πζ/√(1-ζ²)). For robots, overshoot may damage the environment, so a ζ of 0.7 to 1 is common.\n\n### 7.3 Speed loop of a DC motor\nModel the motor-plus-load as G(s) = K/(τs + 1), say K = 200 rpm/V and τ = 0.05 s. Proportional control with K_p = 0.04 V/rpm gives a closed-loop DC gain of K_p K/(1 + K_p K) = 8/9 = 0.889 (steady-state error 11 percent) and a faster time constant τ/(1 + K_p K) = 5.6 ms. Adding integral action removes the steady-state error. For an integrator-plus-lag plant 1/(s(s + 1)) with gain 10, the phase margin is only 18°, so the response is oscillatory: lower the gain or add derivative (lead) action.\n\n### 7.4 Practical issues\nActuator saturation and **integrator anti-windup**, friction (stiction, Coulomb) and backlash, sensor noise (filter the derivative), sampling delay, quantization, flexible links and resonances (notch filters), and safety limits (torque, speed, workspace). Impedance and admittance control let a robot behave like a spring-damper for contact tasks, and force control is needed for assembly, polishing and human interaction.\n\n## Chapter 8. Mobile Robot Kinematics\n\n### 8.1 Differential drive\nTwo wheels of radius r separated by b: wheel speeds ω_L and ω_R give linear velocity v = r(ω_R + ω_L)/2 and angular velocity ω = r(ω_R - ω_L)/b. With r = 0.05 m, b = 0.3 m, ω_L = 10 and ω_R = 12 rad/s: v = **0.55 m/s**, ω = **0.333 rad/s**, turning radius R = v/ω = **1.65 m**. Odometry integrates: x ← x + v cos θ Δt, y ← y + v sin θ Δt, θ ← θ + ω Δt. After 1 s (ten 0.1 s Euler steps) the pose is (0.541, 0.082) m, 19.1°; the exact circular arc gives (0.540, 0.091) m, showing small integration error. Errors in wheel radius, wheel base and slip make odometry drift without bound, so correct it with other sensors.\n\n### 8.2 Other drives\n**Ackermann (car-like)** steering: turning radius R = L/tan δ; with wheelbase L = 0.4 m and steering angle 20°, R = **1.10 m**; non-holonomic, it cannot move sideways. **Omnidirectional** (mecanum, omni wheels) robots move in any direction. Differential and Ackermann robots are **non-holonomic**: they have fewer controllable DOF locally (2) than configuration variables (3: x, y, θ), so parking needs manoeuvres. Control methods: pure pursuit, Stanley controller, Lyapunov-based path tracking, model predictive control.\n\n## Chapter 9. Perception and State Estimation\n\n### 9.1 Camera geometry\nPinhole model: u = f X/Z + c_x, v = f Y/Z + c_y. With f = 800 px and principal point (320, 240), a point (0.5, 0.2, 2.0) m projects to **(520, 320) px**. Calibrate intrinsics (focal length, principal point, distortion) with a checkerboard. **Stereo depth** Z = f B/d (baseline B, disparity d): f = 700 px, B = 0.12 m, d = 21 px gives **Z = 4.0 m**; a one-pixel disparity error changes the depth to 4.2 m, so depth error grows with the square of distance. Visual odometry, feature matching and bundle adjustment recover motion from images.\n\n### 9.2 The Kalman filter for localization\nPredict with the motion model, correct with measurements. 1D example: position x with process noise Q = 0.1 and measurement noise R = 0.5, starting at x = 0, P = 1, commanded motion 1 m per step, measurements 0.9, 2.2, 2.8: gains K = 0.6875, 0.470, 0.401; estimates 0.931, 2.058, 2.954; variances 0.344, 0.235, 0.201. Nonlinear models use the **extended Kalman filter (EKF)** or unscented filter; multi-modal beliefs use **particle filters** (Monte Carlo localization): sample poses, weight them by measurement likelihood, resample.\n\n### 9.3 Mapping and SLAM\nAn **occupancy grid** stores the probability that each cell is occupied; update in log-odds so that evidence adds: l = ln(p/(1 - p)). Starting from 0.5, three \"occupied\" measurements with p = 0.7 give cell probabilities 0.70, 0.84, 0.93; a following \"free\" reading of 0.4 reduces it to 0.89. **SLAM** (simultaneous localization and mapping) estimates the map and pose together (EKF-SLAM, particle-filter SLAM, graph-based SLAM with loop closure, visual SLAM, LiDAR SLAM). Loop closure corrects accumulated drift when the robot recognizes a revisited place.\n\n### 9.4 Sensor fusion\nCombine odometry (smooth, drifts), IMU (fast, drifts), GNSS (absolute, noisy, slow) and LiDAR/vision (absolute relative to the map). Use an EKF/UKF or factor graphs. Always synchronize timestamps and calibrate extrinsic transforms (the transform between sensor frames).\n\n## Chapter 10. Planning and Navigation\n\n### 10.1 Path planning\nRepresent the world as a grid or graph and search. **Dijkstra** expands nodes in order of cost; **A\\*** adds a heuristic h (admissible: never overestimates, such as Manhattan distance on a 4-connected grid) and expands far fewer nodes. Example 5×8 grid with walls: the optimal path length is 13 steps (Manhattan lower bound 11); Dijkstra expanded 29 nodes and A\\* only 20. For high-dimensional configuration spaces (arms) use sampling-based planners: **PRM** (probabilistic roadmaps) and **RRT/RRT\\*** (rapidly exploring random trees), then smooth the path.\n\n### 10.2 Local planning and obstacle avoidance\nPotential fields (attraction to the goal, repulsion from obstacles: can trap the robot in local minima), the **dynamic window approach** (choose a velocity within acceleration and obstacle limits that maximizes progress and clearance), model predictive control, and reactive behaviours. A navigation stack combines a global planner, a local planner, costmaps (inflated obstacles) and recovery behaviours.\n\n### 10.3 Decision making\nFinite-state machines and **behaviour trees** organize tasks (go to a door, open it, pass through); task planners (PDDL) and reinforcement learning handle high-level choices. Always include a safe fallback behaviour (stop, retreat).\n\n## Chapter 11. Robot Software and Safety\n\n### 11.1 ROS 2 and software architecture\nThe Robot Operating System provides nodes that communicate via **topics** (publish/subscribe), **services** (request/response) and **actions** (long-running tasks with feedback); the **tf** tree tracks coordinate frames; simulators (Gazebo, Webots, Isaac Sim) let you test before touching hardware. Keep hard real-time loops (motor control) on microcontrollers or a real-time kernel; run perception and planning on a more powerful computer. Log everything (rosbag) for debugging.\n\n### 11.2 Safety\nHazards: crushing, collisions, electric shock, stored energy, unexpected motion. Layers: mechanical design (guards, force limits), **emergency stop** that removes power independently of software, safety-rated controllers, speed and separation monitoring, power and force limiting for cobots. Standards: ISO 10218 (industrial robots), ISO/TS 15066 (collaborative), ISO 13849 and IEC 62061 (safety-related control), IEC 61508. Perform a risk assessment (severity, exposure, avoidance) and verify safety functions.\n\n## Chapter 12. Design Method and Mistakes Checklist\n1. Specify the task: payload, workspace, speed, accuracy, environment, safety, budget.\n2. Choose the kinematic structure; verify reach and singularities with forward/inverse kinematics.\n3. Compute torques and speeds from the dynamics and trajectory; select motors, gearboxes and drivers with margins.\n4. Select sensors (resolution, rate, noise) and design the control hierarchy; tune inner loops first.\n5. Add estimation (filters), planning and safety; simulate; then test on hardware in stages with an E-stop.\n6. Measure performance (accuracy, repeatability, settling time) and iterate.\n\n**Common mistakes.** Mixing rotation order conventions; forgetting that IK has multiple solutions or none; operating near singularities; ignoring stall current and motor heating; under-sizing gearboxes and ignoring backlash; tuning the outer loop before the inner loop; no anti-windup; trusting odometry without correction; ignoring sensor timestamps and frames; sampling the wrong rate; no hardware emergency stop; and testing a new controller at full power.\n","plan":"# Robotics and Mechatronics: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 10 to 13 hours per week (3 reading or lectures, 7 to 9 simulation, building and testing, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Robotics is learned by integrating: compute the kinematics, simulate it, then drive a real motor with your own code. A modest kit (microcontroller, two geared DC motors with encoders, motor driver, IMU, a distance sensor, a small servo arm) is enough for almost every lab.\n\n## Textbooks\n- John Craig, *Introduction to Robotics: Mechanics and Control* (the standard for kinematics and dynamics); Mark Spong, Seth Hutchinson and M. Vidyasagar, *Robot Modeling and Control*.\n- Kevin Lynch and Frank Park, *Modern Robotics: Mechanics, Planning, and Control* (free PDF, with code and Coursera lectures).\n- Sebastian Thrun, Wolfram Burgard and Dieter Fox, *Probabilistic Robotics* (filters, localization, SLAM); Roland Siegwart, Illah Nourbakhsh and Davide Scaramuzza, *Introduction to Autonomous Mobile Robots*.\n- Devdas Shetty and Richard Kolk, *Mechatronics System Design*; W. Bolton, *Mechatronics*; Peter Corke, *Robotics, Vision and Control* (Python/MATLAB toolboxes).\n- Steven LaValle, *Planning Algorithms* (free online) for motion planning.\n\n## Free courses and resources\n- Modern Robotics specialization (Northwestern, Coursera); MIT 6.4210 Robotic Manipulation; Stanford CS223A Introduction to Robotics; CMU 16-311 materials.\n- Peter Corke's Robotics Toolbox (Python) and QuantEcon-style notebooks; Brian Douglas's control and robotics videos; Sebastian Thrun's \"Artificial Intelligence for Robotics\" (Udacity).\n- ROS 2 documentation and tutorials; Gazebo, Webots and MuJoCo simulators; The Construct's free ROS lessons.\n- Open hardware: TurtleBot, Pololu 3pi, ESP32 + L298N/TB6612 motor-driver kits, low-cost robot arms (SO-100, uArm).\n- ISO 10218 and ISO/TS 15066 summaries from robot manufacturers' safety guides.\n\n## Tools\nPython (NumPy, SciPy, python-control, Matplotlib), the Robotics Toolbox, ROS 2 with Gazebo, a CAD program (Fusion 360, FreeCAD, Onshape), a microcontroller IDE (PlatformIO or Arduino), an oscilloscope or logic analyser, a lab power supply with current limit, and Git.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Introduction, robot types and system architecture.** DOF, workspace, accuracy versus repeatability, sensors-actuators-computing loop. Lab: build a block diagram of a robot you know and list its specifications.\n\n**Week 2: Sensors.** Encoders, IMU, ultrasonic, LiDAR, cameras, noise and calibration. Lab: read an encoder and compute wheel speed and distance per count; characterize an ultrasonic sensor's accuracy against a ruler.\n\n**Week 3: DC motors and drivers.** Motor model, torque-speed curve, H-bridge and PWM, current limits, thermal limits. Lab: measure Kt, R and no-load speed; plot the torque-speed line.\n\n**Week 4: Gears, steppers, servos and actuator sizing.** Reflected inertia, backlash, holding torque, motor selection. Lab: size a motor and gearbox for a given arm and verify with a measured torque test.\n\n**Week 5: Spatial mathematics.** Rotation matrices, homogeneous transforms, Euler angles, quaternions. Lab: code transform composition in Python; verify RᵀR = I; visualize frames.\n\n**Week 6: Forward and inverse kinematics.** DH parameters, planar and 3-DOF arms, multiple IK solutions, workspace. Lab: implement FK/IK for a two-link arm and compare with a servo arm; verify both elbow configurations.\n\n**Week 7: Midterm and Jacobians.** Review. Velocity kinematics, singularities, manipulability, statics (τ = Jᵀ F). Lab: compute J numerically and plot the determinant over the workspace.\n\n**Week 8: Dynamics and trajectories.** Gravity and inertia torques, Lagrange/Newton-Euler overview, cubic, quintic and trapezoidal profiles. Lab: generate trajectories and compute required torque along them.\n\n**Week 9: Control.** Cascade loops, PID tuning, second-order specifications, anti-windup, feedforward, gain and phase margin. Lab: tune a speed loop on a real motor; record step responses and compare with the model.\n\n**Week 10: Mobile robot kinematics and odometry.** Differential drive, Ackermann, non-holonomic constraints, pure pursuit. Lab: drive a square and measure the closure error; calibrate wheel radius and base.\n\n**Week 11: State estimation.** Kalman filter, EKF, particle filter, sensor fusion, IMU attitude estimation. Lab: implement a 1D Kalman filter, then a complementary filter for pitch.\n\n**Week 12: Perception and mapping.** Camera model, stereo depth, occupancy grids, scan matching, SLAM overview. Lab: build an occupancy grid from simulated LiDAR data in a notebook.\n\n**Week 13: Planning, behaviours and software.** Dijkstra, A*, RRT, dynamic window approach, behaviour trees, ROS 2 nodes/topics/tf. Lab: run navigation in Gazebo and tune the costmap.\n\n**Week 14: Safety, integration and project.** Risk assessment, emergency stop, ISO standards, testing methods. Final project demonstration (a mobile base or arm performing a task with measured accuracy) and past-paper revision.\n\n## Assessment pattern used in this course\nThree assignments (spatial description and manipulator kinematics; actuators, sensors, dynamics and trajectories; mobile robots, control, estimation and planning), a class test, and a three-hour final. Practise by implementing each worked example in code and then changing the parameters.\n\n## Study tips\n- Always draw the frames and the sign conventions before writing any equation.\n- Check kinematics numerically: compute forward kinematics of your inverse-kinematics answer.\n- Respect hardware limits: set current limits on the supply, test at low speed first, and keep an emergency stop within reach.\n- Tune inner loops first (current, then velocity, then position) and log step responses.\n- Calibrate every sensor and keep a record of the transform between sensor frames.\n","test":"# Robotics and Mechatronics: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed, no notes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Useful: planar two-link arm x = l₁ cos θ₁ + l₂ cos(θ₁ + θ₂), y = l₁ sin θ₁ + l₂ sin(θ₁ + θ₂); DC motor T = Kt I, V = I R + Ke ω; differential drive v = r(ω_R + ω_L)/2, ω = r(ω_R - ω_L)/b.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Mechatronics is best described as\nA) mechanical design only  B) the integration of mechanics, electronics, control and software  C) software only  D) a type of motor\n\n**2.** To place a tool anywhere in 3D space with any orientation, a serial arm needs at least\nA) 4 joints  B) 5 joints  C) 3 joints  D) 6 joints\n\n**3.** A 1,024-line quadrature encoder gives how many counts per revolution?\nA) 4,096  B) 1,024  C) 512  D) 2,048\n\n**4.** An ultrasonic sensor measuring the round-trip echo time t gives the distance\nA) c t  B) c/t  C) c t/2  D) 2 c t\n\n**5.** The stall torque of a DC motor is\nA) Ke R/V  B) Kt V/R  C) J ω  D) V/Ke\n\n**6.** A gearbox with ratio N and efficiency η multiplies motor torque by\nA) N  B) η/N  C) η N  D) N/η\n\n**7.** Inertia reflected from the load to the motor through a gearbox of ratio N is\nA) J_L N  B) J_L/N  C) J_L N²  D) J_L/N²\n\n**8.** A rotation matrix R satisfies\nA) R = Rᵀ always  B) RᵀR = I and det R = +1  C) det R = -1  D) det R = 0\n\n**9.** Rotations in 3D\nA) cannot be composed  B) are only defined in 2D  C) always commute  D) in general do not commute\n\n**10.** Gimbal lock is a problem of\nA) encoders  B) Euler-angle representations  C) rotation matrices  D) quaternions\n\n**11.** For a two-link arm the inverse kinematics generally has\nA) no solution  B) infinitely many solutions always  C) exactly one solution  D) up to two solutions (elbow up and elbow down) inside the workspace\n\n**12.** A kinematic singularity occurs where the Jacobian\nA) has all positive entries  B) loses rank (det J = 0)  C) is symmetric  D) equals the identity\n\n**13.** Joint torques needed to support an end-effector force F are\nA) τ = F/J  B) τ = Jᵀ F  C) τ = J⁻¹ F²  D) τ = J F\n\n**14.** A cubic trajectory with zero start and end velocity has peak velocity\nA) 1.5 Δq/T  B) Δq/T  C) 3 Δq/T  D) 0.5 Δq/T\n\n**15.** A quintic trajectory is preferred over a cubic one because it\nA) specifies acceleration as well, giving smoother motion  B) avoids all errors  C) is shorter  D) needs no computation\n\n**16.** Overshoot of a second-order system depends mainly on\nA) the sensor mass  B) the sample rate only  C) the damping ratio ζ  D) the cable length\n\n**17.** Integrator anti-windup is needed when\nA) the system has no feedback  B) the motor is stepper  C) the actuator saturates while the integrator keeps accumulating  D) the encoder is absolute\n\n**18.** A differential-drive robot with both wheels at the same speed moves\nA) in place  B) in a circle  C) in a straight line  D) sideways\n\n**19.** Dead-reckoning odometry drifts because of\nA) wireless interference only  B) an absolute encoder  C) too much light  D) wheel slip and calibration errors accumulating\n\n**20.** A* is guaranteed to find an optimal path if the heuristic is\nA) larger than the true cost  B) admissible (never overestimates)  C) random  D) always zero\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Transforms (10 marks)** (a) Rotate the vector (1, 2, 0) about the z axis by 90° [3]. (b) A frame is rotated 90° about z and translated by (3, 0, 0). Find the coordinates in the base frame of the point (1, 2, 0) given in the moving frame [4]. (c) Find the inverse transform of the rotation Rz(90°) [3].\n\n**B2. Two-link arm (10 marks)** Both links are 1 m long. (a) Find the tip position for θ₁ = 0, θ₂ = 90° [3]. (b) Solve the inverse kinematics for the target (1, 1) (both solutions) [4]. (c) At θ₁ = 0, θ₂ = 90° give the Jacobian and its determinant, and say where the singular configurations are [3].\n\n**B3. DC motor and gearbox (10 marks)** A motor has V = 12 V, R = 3 Ω, Kt = Ke = 0.1 N·m/A. (a) Find the no-load speed (rpm), stall torque and stall current [4]. (b) For a 0.1 N·m load find current, speed (rpm) and efficiency [4]. (c) A 20:1 gearbox with 90 percent efficiency is attached; find output torque for the 0.1 N·m motor torque [2].\n\n**B4. Trajectory and mobile robot (10 marks)** (a) Find the cubic coefficients, peak velocity and peak acceleration for a 90° joint move in 3 s from rest to rest [4]. (b) A differential-drive robot has r = 0.05 m and b = 0.2 m. Find v, ω and turning radius for ω_L = 8 rad/s, ω_R = 12 rad/s [4]. (c) What happens if ω_L = ω_R = 10 rad/s? [2]\n\n## Answer key and solutions\n\n### Section A\n1. B 2. D 3. A 4. C 5. B 6. C 7. D 8. B 9. D 10. B 11. D 12. B 13. B 14. A 15. A 16. C 17. C 18. C 19. D 20. B\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Rz(90°)(x, y, z) = (-y, x, z), so (1, 2, 0) → **(-2, 1, 0)** [3].\n(b) p_base = R p + t = (-2, 1, 0) + (3, 0, 0) = **(1, 1, 0)** [4].\n(c) Inverse of a rotation is its transpose: Rz(90°)⁻¹ = Rz(-90°) = [[0, 1, 0], [-1, 0, 0], [0, 0, 1]] [3]. (For a full transform [R t; 0 1] the inverse is [Rᵀ -Rᵀt; 0 1].)\n\n**B2.**\n(a) x = cos 0 + cos 90° = **1**; y = sin 0 + sin 90° = **1**: tip at **(1, 1)** [3].\n(b) cos θ₂ = (1 + 1 - 1 - 1)/2 = 0, so θ₂ = ±90°. θ₂ = +90°: θ₁ = atan2(1, 1) - atan2(1, 1 + 0) = 45° - 45° = **0°**. θ₂ = -90°: θ₁ = 45° + 45° = **90°** [4].\n(c) J = [[-sin θ₁ - sin(θ₁ + θ₂), -sin(θ₁ + θ₂)], [cos θ₁ + cos(θ₁ + θ₂), cos(θ₁ + θ₂)]] = [[-1, -1], [1, 0]]; det J = 0 + 1 = **1** (= l₁ l₂ sin θ₂ = 1) [2]. Singular configurations: θ₂ = 0° (arm stretched, reach 2 m) and θ₂ = 180° (folded, reach 0) [1].\n\n**B3.**\n(a) ω₀ = V/Ke = 120 rad/s = **1,146 rpm**; T_stall = Kt V/R = 0.1 × 12/3 = **0.4 N·m**; I_stall = V/R = **4 A** [4].\n(b) I = T/Kt = **1 A**; ω = (V - I R)/Ke = (12 - 3)/0.1 = 90 rad/s = **859 rpm**; efficiency = T ω/(V I) = 0.1 × 90/12 = **75 percent** [4].\n(c) T_out = 0.1 × 20 × 0.9 = **1.8 N·m** (speed 859/20 = 43 rpm) [2].\n\n**B4.**\n(a) a₂ = 3Δq/T² = 270/9 = **30 °/s²**; a₃ = -2Δq/T³ = -180/27 = **-6.67 °/s³**; peak velocity = 1.5 × 90/3 = **45 °/s**; peak acceleration = 6 × 90/9 = **60 °/s²** [4].\n(b) v = 0.05 × (8 + 12)/2 = **0.5 m/s**; ω = 0.05 × (12 - 8)/0.2 = **1.0 rad/s**; R = v/ω = **0.5 m** [4].\n(c) With equal wheel speeds ω = 0 and v = 0.05 × 10 = 0.5 m/s: the robot moves in a **straight line** at 0.5 m/s [2].\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Mechatronics is best described as","options":["mechanical design only","the integration of mechanics, electronics, control and software","software only","a type of motor"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"To place a tool anywhere in 3D space with any orientation, a serial arm needs at least","options":["4 joints","5 joints","3 joints","6 joints"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A 1,024-line quadrature encoder gives how many counts per revolution?","options":["4,096","1,024","512","2,048"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An ultrasonic sensor measuring the round-trip echo time t gives the distance","options":["c t","c/t","c t/2","2 c t"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The stall torque of a DC motor is","options":["Ke R/V","Kt V/R","J ω","V/Ke"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A gearbox with ratio N and efficiency η multiplies motor torque by","options":["N","η/N","η N","N/η"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Inertia reflected from the load to the motor through a gearbox of ratio N is","options":["J_L N","J_L/N","J_L N²","J_L/N²"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A rotation matrix R satisfies","options":["R = Rᵀ always","RᵀR = I and det R = +1","det R = -1","det R = 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Rotations in 3D","options":["cannot be composed","are only defined in 2D","always commute","in general do not commute"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Gimbal lock is a problem of","options":["encoders","Euler-angle representations","rotation matrices","quaternions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"For a two-link arm the inverse kinematics generally has","options":["no solution","infinitely many solutions always","exactly one solution","up to two solutions (elbow up and elbow down) inside the workspace"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A kinematic singularity occurs where the Jacobian","options":["has all positive entries","loses rank (det J = 0)","is symmetric","equals the identity"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Joint torques needed to support an end-effector force F are","options":["τ = F/J","τ = Jᵀ F","τ = J⁻¹ F²","τ = J F"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A cubic trajectory with zero start and end velocity has peak velocity","options":["1.5 Δq/T","Δq/T","3 Δq/T","0.5 Δq/T"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A quintic trajectory is preferred over a cubic one because it","options":["specifies acceleration as well, giving smoother motion","avoids all errors","is shorter","needs no computation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Overshoot of a second-order system depends mainly on","options":["the sensor mass","the sample rate only","the damping ratio ζ","the cable length"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Integrator anti-windup is needed when","options":["the system has no feedback","the motor is stepper","the actuator saturates while the integrator keeps accumulating","the encoder is absolute"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A differential-drive robot with both wheels at the same speed moves","options":["in place","in a circle","in a straight line","sideways"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Dead-reckoning odometry drifts because of","options":["wireless interference only","an absolute encoder","too much light","wheel slip and calibration errors accumulating"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A* is guaranteed to find an optimal path if the heuristic is","options":["larger than the true cost","admissible (never overestimates)","random","always zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A robot differs from an automated machine mainly because it","options":["has a motor","uses electricity","has wheels","senses its environment and makes decisions to act"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The repeatability of a robot refers to","options":["how closely it returns to the same commanded pose","its weight","its maximum payload","its top speed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The correct relation between a gearbox's output speed and torque is","options":["speed falls by the ratio and torque rises by the ratio (less losses)","neither changes","both increase","both decrease"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A stepper motor driven open loop can","options":["lose steps if overloaded","never lose steps","measure its own position","exceed stall torque"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The mechanical time constant of a DC motor is","options":["Kt/Ke","L/R","J/B²","R J/(Ke Kt)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A homogeneous transform matrix is a","options":["4 × 4 matrix with a rotation and a translation","1 × 6 vector","3 × 3 rotation only","2 × 2 matrix"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Quaternions are preferred for interpolation of orientation because they","options":["have no singularity and interpolate smoothly (slerp)","are Euler angles","use fewer multiplications always","are easier to read"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The workspace of a planar two-link arm with unrestricted joints is","options":["an annulus between |l₁ - l₂| and l₁ + l₂","a cube","a line","a point"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Jacobian relates","options":["mass to inertia","joint velocities to end-effector velocities","sensor noise to range","joint torques to currents"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Near a singularity the joint velocities needed for a tip velocity","options":["become negative","become zero","become very large","are unaffected"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A trapezoidal velocity profile has","options":["constant acceleration, a cruise at constant velocity, then constant deceleration","constant jerk","a sine-wave shape","zero velocity"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The damping ratio for no overshoot in a second-order system is","options":["ζ = 0","ζ < 0.1","ζ ≥ 1","ζ = 0.5"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Feedforward control in robotics is used to","options":["add noise","remove the need for a model","replace sensors","supply the expected torque (gravity, acceleration) so feedback only corrects errors"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a differential-drive robot, unequal wheel speeds produce","options":["a curved path with radius R = v/ω","no motion","only straight motion","sideways motion"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A non-holonomic robot such as a car","options":["cannot translate sideways and needs manoeuvres to park","has no kinematics","can move in any direction instantly","has no wheels"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Kalman gain decreases as the estimate variance","options":["is infinite","decreases","is unrelated","increases"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In an occupancy grid using log-odds, repeated \"occupied\" evidence","options":["resets the cell","adds, increasing the occupied log-odds","is ignored","subtracts"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A* with an admissible heuristic","options":["needs negative costs","finds an optimal path while usually expanding fewer nodes than Dijkstra","never terminates","is faster than Dijkstra but not optimal"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A hardware emergency stop must","options":["remove power to the actuators independently of the control software","work only when the network is up","be optional","depend on the robot software"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In ROS, long-running goals with feedback and cancellation use","options":["bags","topics","actions","parameters only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
